This section covers Row Grouping in the Server-Side Row Model (SSRM).

## Grouping Rows on the Server

Set the `rowGroup` column definition property on each column to group by. The example below groups rows by
**Country**:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'country', rowGroup: true },
        { field: 'athlete' },
        { field: 'sport' },
        { field: 'year' },
    ]
}
```

{% gridExampleRunner title="Enabling Row Grouping" name="enabling-row-grouping" /%}

The server groups the rows. When the grid needs rows, it calls `getRows(params)` on the
[Server-Side Datasource](./server-side-model-datasource/), and `params.request` carries the grouping state:

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="IServerSideGetRowsRequest" names=["rowGroupCols", "groupKeys"] config={"description": ""} /%}

For example, when the grid groups by **Country** then **Sport**, and the user expands **Argentina**, the request is:

```js
{
    rowGroupCols: [
        { id: 'country', field: 'country', displayName: 'Country' },
        { id: 'sport', field: 'sport', displayName: 'Sport' },
    ],
    groupKeys: ['Argentina'],
    // ...
}
```

### Returning Group Rows

Each request loads the children of one group, grouped by the column `rowGroupCols[groupKeys.length]`:

- With no `groupKeys`, return the top-level group rows, one per distinct value of the first row group column.
- With some `groupKeys`, keep only the rows matching those keys, and return one group row per distinct value of the
  next row group column.
- When `groupKeys` has as many keys as `rowGroupCols`, return the matching leaf rows.

The grid reads the key of a group row from its data, through the group column's `field` or `valueGetter`, and sends
it in the `groupKeys` of later requests for that group's children. The request above can be answered with:

```js
params.success({
    rowData: [
        { sport: 'Basketball' },
        { sport: 'Cycling' },
        // ...
    ],
    // the number of children of 'Argentina'
    rowCount: 9,
});
```

The `rowCount` is the number of rows in that one group. Page within a group with `startRow` and `endRow`, as described
in [Server-Side Datasource](./server-side-model-datasource/). Return aggregated values on group rows as described in
[SSRM Aggregation](./server-side-model-aggregation/). With [Row IDs](./server-side-model-configuration/#providing-row-ids),
group rows need [unique IDs](./server-side-model-configuration/#supplying-unique-group-ids) too.

The example below groups by two levels. Note the following:

- **Country** and **Sport** set `rowGroup: true`, so there are two levels of grouping.
- The server returns one group row per Country, then one per Sport when a Country is expanded, then the leaf rows when a Sport is expanded.
- The group column shows the **Athlete** of each leaf row, as `autoGroupColumnDef` sets `field: 'athlete'`.
- Open the browser's dev console to view the request supplied to the datasource.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Row Grouping" name="row-grouping"  exampleHeight=590 /%}

## Showing Child Counts on Group Rows

To show a row count beside each group name, implement the `getChildCount(dataItem)` callback. The grid does not know
how many rows a group holds, so return the count from the group row's data, which the server can include.

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-options/properties.json" section="serverSideRowModel" names=["getChildCount"] /%}

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    getChildCount: data => {
        // here child count is stored in the 'childCount' property
        return data.childCount;
    }
}
```

{% gridExampleRunner title="Child Counts" name="child-counts"  exampleHeight=590 /%}

## Opening Groups by Default

To open groups as soon as they load, implement the `isServerSideGroupOpenByDefault` callback.

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-options/properties.json" section="serverSideRowModel" names=["isServerSideGroupOpenByDefault"] /%}

```js
// Example implementation
function isServerSideGroupOpenByDefault(params) {
    var rowNode = params.rowNode;
    var isZimbabwe = rowNode.field == 'country' && rowNode.key == 'Zimbabwe';
    return isZimbabwe;
}
```

{% note %}
Server-Side Open By Default requires [Row IDs](./server-side-model-configuration/#providing-row-ids) to be supplied to the grid.
{% /note %}

Use the [Row Node](./row-object/) method `getRoute()` to inspect the route of a row node.

{% apiDocumentation source="row-object/properties.json" section="serverSide" names=["getRoute"] /%}

Below shows `isServerSideGroupOpenByDefault()` and `getRoute` in action. Note the following:

- The callback opens the following routes as soon as those routes are loaded:
    - **\[Zimbabwe]**
    - **\[Zimbabwe, Swimming]**
    - **\[United States, Swimming]**
- **\[Zimbabwe]** and **\[Zimbabwe, Swimming]** are visibly open.
- **\[United States, Swimming]** is not visibly open, as **United States** is closed. Opening **United States** shows its **Swimming** group open.
- Selecting a group row and clicking 'Route of Selected' prints the route to the selected node to the developer console.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Open by Default" name="open-by-default" /%}

## Hiding Expanded Group Rows

To hide a group row while it is expanded, set the `groupHideOpenParents` property to `true`.

In the example below, expanding a group replaces the group row with its first child. Collapsing it shows the group row
again.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Hide Open Parents" name="hide-open-parents" /%}

{% note %}
`groupHideOpenParents=true` turns off [Sticky Groups](./grouping-opening-groups/#prevent-sticky-groups) and [Full Width Loading](./server-side-model-loading-rows/#full-width-loading-row).
{% /note %}

## Unbalanced Groups with Empty Keys

To support unbalanced groups, where some rows have no value for a group column, set the `groupAllowUnbalanced`
property to `true`. A group with the key `''` is then always expanded, and its group row is hidden.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Unbalanced Groups" name="unbalanced-groups" /%}

{% note %}
With `groupAllowUnbalanced=true`, a hidden row group still holds the unbalanced rows. Consider it when working with
selection state, refreshing or group paths. Loading these rows also takes an extra request, as they belong to the
hidden group.
{% /note %}

## Expanding and Collapsing All Groups

The `expandAll()` and `collapseAll()` grid API methods apply only to **loaded group rows** by default. To include groups
that are not loaded yet, set `ssrmExpandAllAffectsAllRows: true`.

In the example below, note the following:

- The first button expands all loaded group rows.
- The checkbox sets `ssrmExpandAllAffectsAllRows`. The first button then also expands groups that are not loaded yet.
- The second button collapses all group rows.

{% gridExampleRunner title="expand-all-affects-all-rows" name="expand-all-affects-all-rows"  exampleHeight=540 /%}

To open only some groups, such as the top level, call `forEachNode()` and `setExpanded()`:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
// Expand all top level row nodes
api.forEachNode(node => {
    if (node.group && node.level == 0) {
        node.setExpanded(true);
    }
});
```

The example below demonstrates these techniques. Note the following:

- **Expand All** expands the loaded group rows. On first load that is every **Year** group. Clicking it again, once
  they have loaded, also expands their **Country** groups: hundreds of rows.
- **Collapse All** collapses all rows.
- **Expand Top Level Only** expands **Year** groups only, even when more group rows are loaded.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Expand All" name="expand-all" /%}

## Grouping by Nested Object Fields

When a grouped column holds an object, read the group key from one of its fields with a `valueGetter` or a `field` in
dot notation. In the example below, each row holds **Country** as an object:

```js
const rowData = {
    country: {
        name: 'Ireland',
        code: 'IRE'
    },
    // other fields as normal
}
```

**Country** uses a `valueGetter` that reads `data.country.name`.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Complex Objects" name="complex-objects"  exampleHeight=590 /%}

## Filtering with Row Groups

Changing a filter reloads the whole grid by default. To reload only the groups the filter affects, set
`serverSideOnlyRefreshFilteredGroups`. The grid does not reload the groups above them, so a group row can be left with
no children.

In the example below, note the following:

- Filtering by `Gold`, `Silver` or `Bronze` fully purges the grid, as they are [aggregated](./server-side-model-aggregation/) and every group row shows a value for them.
- Filtering `Year` reloads only the `Year` group rows, as the example sets `serverSideOnlyRefreshFilteredGroups`.
- Filtering `Year` to `1900` leaves every group with no leaf rows.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Filtering" name="filtering"  exampleHeight=590 /%}

## Deferring Columns Tool Panel Changes

To stage changes in the Columns Tool Panel and send one request when the user clicks **Apply**, include the **Apply**
button in `toolPanelParams.buttons`.

Note that in the example below:

- Changes made in the Columns Tool Panel are staged as pending changes.
- **Apply** commits all pending changes in a single operation.
- **Cancel** discards all pending changes and restores the last applied state.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Deferred Updates" name="deferred-apply-mode"  exampleHeight=830 /%}

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    sideBar: {
        toolPanels: [
            {
                id: 'columns',
                labelDefault: 'Columns',
                labelKey: 'columns',
                iconKey: 'columns',
                toolPanel: 'agColumnsToolPanel',
                toolPanelParams: {
                    buttons: ['cancel', 'apply'],
                },
            },
        ],
        defaultToolPanel: 'columns',
    },
};
```

{% note %}
Changes made outside the Columns Tool Panel — such as dragging columns into the Row Group or Pivot Panels, using the Column Menu, or calling the Grid / Column API — are applied immediately and clear any pending changes. Column pinning, resizing, and group expansion do not clear pending changes.
{% /note %}
